1 . Contains Duplicate :
Given an integer array nums, return true if any value appears more than once in the array, otherwise return false.
Example 1:

Input: nums = [1, 2, 3, 3]

Output: true

Example 2:

Input: nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]

Output: false

Thinking :

1. Brute Force Approach : 

public boolean containsDuplicate(int[] nums) {
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {  // start from i+1 to avoid self-comparison
            if (nums[i] == nums[j]) {
                return true; // duplicate found
            }
        }
    }
    return false; // no duplicates
}


// This is called brute force thinking

🔎 Time Complexity Analysis

Outer loop (i from 0 to n-1): runs n times.

Inner loop (j from i+1 to n-1):

When i = 0, inner loop runs ~n times.

When i = 1, inner loop runs ~n-1 times.

When i = 2, inner loop runs ~n-2 times.

…

When i = n-1, inner loop runs 0 times.

So the total number of comparisons is:
(n−1)+(n−2)+(n−3)+...+1

✅ Final Answer

Time Complexity: O(n²)
Because in the worst case (no duplicates), we check almost every pair of elements.

Space Complexity: O(1)
No extra data structures used.

⚡ That’s why for large nums (like length = 10⁵), this brute force approach becomes too slow and we prefer the HashSet solution (O(n)).


Optimal Approach :

import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static boolean hasDuplicates(int[] nums) {  // made static
        HashSet<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();
        for (int num : nums) {  // fixed enhanced for loop
            if (seen.contains(num)) {
                return true;
            }
            seen.add(num);
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] nums = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            nums[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }

        boolean result = hasDuplicates(nums);  // returns boolean
        System.out.println(result);  // print result
    }
}

// Understanding : Optimal Approach 
// HashSet Properties

// Checking if an element exists (seen.contains(num)) is O(1) on average.

// Adding an element (seen.add(num)) is also O(1) on average.

// Looping Through the Array

// We only scan the array once → O(n).
// So overall:

// Time Complexity: O(n) (instead of O(n²))

// Space Complexity: O(n) (to store seen elements in HashSet)

// nums = [1, 2, 3, 3]

// Steps:

// seen = {}

// Read 1: not seen → add → seen = {1}

// Read 2: not seen → add → seen = {1,2}

// Read 3: not seen → add → seen = {1,2,3}

// Read 3: already in seen → return true

// Why is it Optimal?

// Brute force → checks every pair (wasteful).

// HashSet → remembers what we’ve seen, so we only check once per element.

// For very large arrays (say n = 100,000):

// Brute force → ~5,000,000,000 comparisons 😵

// HashSet → just 100,000 checks 🚀


1.1. Brute Force (O(n²), O(1))

✅ Advantage: No extra memory.

❌ Disadvantage: Extremely slow for large arrays.

🚫 Rarely acceptable in interviews (only for learning basics).

2. HashSet (O(n), O(n))

✅ Advantage: Fastest in practice (linear time).

✅ Easy to implement.

❌ Uses extra memory (up to O(n)).

⭐ Best general-purpose solution (this is what most candidates use in LeetCode/Interviews).

3. Sorting (O(n log n), O(1))

✅ Advantage: No extra memory (apart from sorting overhead).

❌ Slower than HashSet because of sorting.

⭐ Best when the interviewer says:

“Can you solve it without extra memory?”


4. Frequency Array / Bitset (O(n), O(range))

✅ Advantage: Super fast (linear).

❌ Disadvantage: Needs numbers to be within a small known range (like 0 ≤ num ≤ 1e6).

⭐ Best in competitive programming or special constraints problems.

Final Verdict

In real-world coding interviews (LeetCode style):
👉 HashSet (O(n)) is the best approach.
Because it balances speed + simplicity.

If interviewer asks “no extra memory”:
👉 Use Sorting (O(n log n), O(1)).

If numbers are small and range-limited:
👉 Use Frequency Array / Bitset (O(n), O(range)).


Approch : 3 = > when no memory is used :

import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static boolean hasDuplicates(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums); // O(n log n)
        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] nums = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            nums[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }
        System.out.println(hasDuplicates(nums));
    }
}

4.Frequency Array / Boolean Array (O(n), O(maxValue) space) :

Works only if you know the range of numbers (like 0 ≤ nums[i] ≤ 10⁶).

Create a boolean array of size maxValue + 1.

Mark each number as seen.

If a number is already seen, return true.

public static boolean hasDuplicates(int[] nums) {
    int maxVal = 1000000; // assume numbers are ≤ 1e6
    boolean[] seen = new boolean[maxVal + 1];
    for (int num : nums) {
        if (seen[num]) {
            return true;
        }
        seen[num] = true;
    }
    return false;
}

✅ Summary of Approaches:

Brute force: O(n²), O(1) → not practical.

HashSet: O(n), O(n) → best general solution.

Sorting: O(n log n), O(1) → good when you want to avoid extra memory.

Frequency array / Bitset: O(n), O(range) → best when values are small and range is known.
